  "reasoning": "For current federal races, the only named-candidate federal contest clearly identified for 2026 is U.S. Sen. Jack Reed running for re-election, with local Democratic committees already endorsing him and no evidence of a strong or credible Republican challenger emerging in recent coverage.[7] In the most recent federal elections in 2024, Democrats won Rhode Island\u2019s presidential vote 55.8%\u201342.0% (Harris over Trump, a 13.8\u2011point margin) and the U.S. Senate race 60.0%\u201340.0% (Whitehouse over Morgan, a 20\u2011point margin), indicating a consistent Democratic advantage; using these, a typical recent federal margin is on the order of 15 points in favor of Democrats.[5] Structurally, Rhode Island\u2019s statewide Cook Partisan Voting Index is D+8, confirming a solid Democratic lean at the federal level despite a large share of unaffiliated voters, and there is no recent high-quality polling or rating suggesting a competitive federal race, so the state currently leans Democratic with high confidence based on these recent results.[1][5][14]",
